  frunk.store's changes for the browser are in engine/eduke32-web.patch
  (also tools/duke3d/eduke32-web.patch in
  https://github.com/RobertPKyle/frunk-store, whose tools/build-duke3d.mjs
  builds it with Emscripten 4.0.10), each change marked "[frunk]". Of the
  files changed, only source/build/src/engine.cpp comes from Ken
  Silverman's original release, and it says it has been modified. The
  complete patched source the build came from, with its build script
  (web/build.sh), is engine/eduke32-ec5824d-web-src.tar.gz.
  The changes: the game waits for the page between frames (JSPI) and draws
  once per screen refresh, controllers and touch handed in by the page,
  saving and loading "Continue" from the page, windows smaller than
  640x400, sound mixed in bigger slices, the classic software renderer
  only, and no network play.
  The idea of letting the game's loops wait for the browser came from
  Gawen Arab's EDuke32 WebAssembly work
  (https://voidpoint.io/terminx/eduke32/-/merge_requests/365).

Game (shareware/)
  Duke Nukem 3D Episode One (L.A. Meltdown), shareware version 1.3D,
  Copyright 1996 3D Realms Entertainment, a division of Apogee Software.
  shareware/3dduke13SW.zip is the shareware as 3D Realms released it
  (its 1998 repack, with the license dated 7.16.98), complete and
  unchanged (MD5 04e4ca70b8a2d59ed56c451c5c1d5d39, SHA-256
  c67efd179022bc6d9bde54f404c707cbcbdc15423c20be72e277bc2bdddf3d0e). Its
  license (shareware/LICENSE.TXT, also inside the zip) lets free web
  sites make the game available for download, with all its files and
  nothing added or removed. The page downloads the zip and takes the
  game's data (DUKE3D.GRP and DUKE.RTS) out of it on the player's screen.

